WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.280
"Ultra-Processed Junk Food
Put to the Test"

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.480
In the U.S., the term "junk food" is
often used to describe commonly

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.640
known less-healthy food categories
such as candy, ice cream, and chips.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.240
However, there's no consistent definition,
so nutrition researchers came up

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.440
with the concept of ultra-processed.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.120
The term ultra-processed "food,"
if you want to call it that,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.520
describes industrial formulations that
are typically seen in those long lists

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.880
of ingredients, which besides salt,
sugar, and fat, aren't typically found

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.000
in any cookbook, like the various
flavors, colors, sweeteners,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.280
emulsifiers, and other additives
used to imitate real foods

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.200
or to disguise undesirable
qualities of the final product.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.640
This roughly corresponds
to my idea of red light foods

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.000
in my traffic light system.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.600
And indeed, most of what people eat
are in the red: soda, ice cream, candy,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.160
cakes, most bread and breakfast cereals,
TV dinner-type ready-to-heat products,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.360
chicken nuggets, fish sticks,
sausages, burgers, hot dogs...

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.400
There's been a dramatic rise
in ultra-processed foods.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.040
In fact, the U.S. food supply
is dominated by it.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.280
More than 200,000 products
were assessed

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.680
and 71% were classified
as ultra-processed.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.760
And, of course, they aren't
only in grocery stores.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.320
Sugary drinks and processed junk are
ubiquitous even at non-food retailers,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.720
providing pervasive cues
to consume products

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.920
that are dense in calories,
but poor in nutrition.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.000
As a former president
of Coke put it,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.800
they want to keep Coca-Cola
within an "arm's reach of desire."

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.000
Another major candy brand boasted,
"We put them everywhere:

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.240
grocery stores and supermarkets,
gas stations and chiropractors offices,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.360
bowling alleys and grocery stores—
which we already mentioned.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.365
Not sorry."

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.680
So this is where we are today.
What is the proportion of food

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.840
consumed by U.S. children and
adolescents that's classified as junk?

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.360
An unbelievable 56% to 70% of
what our children and teens eat

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.400
over the entire day is junk.
Okay, yeah, but kids will be kids.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.080
In the United States of America,
more than half of the calories

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.840
taken in across the board is junk.
In fact, around the world

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.200
ultra-processed foods consistently
account for more than 50%

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.480
of the dietary caloric intake
in higher-income countries.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.320
No wonder that unhealthy diets
are humanity's greatest killer,

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.190
the leading risk factor for
death on Planet Earth.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.000
What exactly are the
health consequences?

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.680
The biological effects of modern
foods have been studied on rats,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.960
showing they gorge themselves into
dramatic weight gain, inflammation,

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.280
and metabolic and
cognitive abnormalities.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.680
And just as ultra-processed
foods were taking over,

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.440
a new eating disorder was recognized,
binge eating, which grew into

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.160
the most common form
of eating disorder.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.080
And not surprisingly, binge foods were
found to be 100% ultra-processed.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.400
And I mean, I say no surprise
because these foods are engineered

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.080
so you can't have just one.
People don't tend to binge on broccoli.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.120
About 9 out of 10 studies found that
ultra-processed food consumption

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.440
was associated with adverse
health outcomes, and

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.600
not just obesity but cancer, type-2
diabetes, cardiovascular diseases,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.840
irritable bowel syndrome,
depression, frailty,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.680
all-cause mortality,
meaning living a shorter life.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.480
Studies on youth add asthma to the
list, as well as higher DNA damage.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.800
Not a single study reported
an association between

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.320
ultra-processed foods and
beneficial health outcomes.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.520
In contrast, populations with low
meat consumption and higher fiber

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.520
and minimally processed food intake
have far less chronic diseases,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.040
enjoy lower obesity rates,
and live longer disease-free.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.120
But most findings were derived
from observational studies.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.480
You don't know for sure if
ultra-processed foods themselves

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.760
are to blame,
until you put it to the test.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.280
There's never been a randomized
controlled trial on ultra-processed

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.560
food until now. Twenty people were
essentially locked in a hospital ward

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.560
and received both ultra-processed and
unprocessed diets for 14 days each.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.200
And here's the kicker: diets were
matched for presented calories,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.926
sugar, fat, fiber,
and macronutrients.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.200
See, in response to criticism,
manufacturers are now proposing

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.000
reformulating their products,
keeping them ultra-processed,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.480
but just tweaking them by adding
some fiber, for instance,

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.205
or reducing the sugar, fat, or salt.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.080
So that's why the researchers
wanted to give the study subjects

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.560
the same amount of calories, sugar,
fat, fiber, carbs, and protein in each of

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.880
the two diets in order to try to tease
out the effect of ultra-processing.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.760
So, for instance, instead of giving
people Cheerios and a muffin

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.640
for breakfast in the ultra-processed
weeks, or a cheese and egg

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.960
McMuffin with turkey
bacon and OJ,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.400
they gave people oatmeal
with blueberries and almonds.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.760
Both meals had the same amount
of overall sugar and fat,

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.840
but the unprocessed option was
presented more in whole food form.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.360
For lunch, the ultra-processed group
might get a turkey sandwich

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.480
with nonfat Greek yogurt, canned
peaches, baked potato chips,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.600
and sugar-free Crystal Light lemonade,
versus a Southwest entrée salad

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.400
with black beans, avocados, and
nuts, and grapes and apples

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.840
on the unprocessed diet. The same
amount of calories offered,

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.960
with the instruction to eat as much
or as little as they wanted.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.240
So what happened?

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.680
On the ultra-processed diet, they
ate about 500 more calories a day,

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.120
so, no surprise, they gained about
two pounds on the processed diet,

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.160
or actively lost two pounds
on the less processed diet.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.240
So it wasn't just the unbalanced
nutrient profile of ultra-processed foods,

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.720
so simply tweaking them wouldn't
magically make them healthy.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.400
But that's what the industry would
rather do. Reformulation is referred to

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.960
as an unobtrusive strategy, creating
the prospect of nutritional improvement

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.320
without dietary change. What this study
showed, however, is that it may be better

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:59.120
to limit the consumption of
ultra-processed foods altogether.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.760
Why does the industry love them
so much? They're made

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:05.200
with dirt-cheap ingredients, like
taxpayer-subsidized corn syrup,

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.920
allowing for huge corporate
profit margins. But at what cost?

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:12.800
The food industry takes in more
than a trillion dollars, yet most

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.160
of our healthcare dollars go to treat
chronic disease exacerbated

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.960
by these very same foods, like
diabetes and heart disease;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.760
so you could argue we lose triple
what the food industry pockets.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.680
The industry argues that in
modern societies it's unrealistic

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:30.560
to advise people to avoid
ultra-processed foods

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.560
given societal time constraints
and the difficulties of food prep.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.160
But this may just be acquiescing

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.200
to the same propaganda
and disinformation campaign

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.120
that the processed food industry has
used to co-opt families for decades.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.066
Those who think healthy
foods can't be convenient

00:06:46.066 --> 00:06:50.160
have never met an apple.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.000
That was a response to Dr. Lustig's
essay on processed food

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.440
as a failed experiment. I don't
like his mother-blaming,

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:05.600
but I do appreciate his prescription.
"There's only one recourse—real food."

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:10.740
We need to start thinking
outside of the box.

